From a6b53480d5ef81ad6519d91f8b7df6c1f6b0e94c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: seedds <seedds@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 23:48:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0150: write the HUD local matrix (+0xa0) so the shift
 survives the game's recompose

Pane::Calculate recomposes a pane's global matrix (+0x70) from the LOCAL matrix its +0xa0 points at, every frame -- never from the +0x30/+0x48 components. hud_edge (0147) wrote +0x30/+0x48 and +0x70 but never +0xa0, so the game's next recompose wiped the +0x70 write, the e_mod recompute check mistook the wipe for a game recompose and polluted the stored original (base - tdx), and every later write became a no-op. In #65 not even the startup values applied: pixel measurement showed the elements sitting at the overlay (.bflyt) positions (REPORT-HUD-DRAG-FAIL.md).

This patch stores the original local matrix per entry (e_lm0, entry +128, 12 floats; a NULL +0xa0 skips only the matrix write) and writes it after the +0x70 write, re-derived from e_lm0 with the ABSOLUTE target (tdx/tdy/tk, the 0142 convention), so the game's own recompose computes the shifted global. The restore writes the matrix back to e_lm0 too, and the e_mod recompute check only follows e_mod when the live local matrix still holds the shift (a stale-matrix recompose leaves e_mod alone and the write re-applies). The local-changed check now also fires when the matrix differs from the stored shifted copy, so a scale-only wipe is re-applied, and it follows the local components per-component instead of all four at once.

Table entry 128 -> 176 bytes, HUD_EDGE_SLOTS_PER_ELEM 16 -> 11 (66 entries, state +0x300..+0x3060, short of the diagnostic probe buffer at +0x3300; the 0147 matcher takes one 'All' per layout instance, so eleven slots per element is still several times the realistic maximum). Word table regenerated: 988 words (was 764), md5-stable, first word 0xf100003f.
